{"name":"Hiking Trail","hex":"#a99170","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hiking","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of hike"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Walking in the countryside for pleasure or sport.","The act by which something is hiked, or raised sharply."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hiking"},{"term":"trail","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To follow behind (someone or something); to tail (someone or something).","To drag (something) behind on the ground."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The track or indication marking the route followed by something that has passed, such as the footprints of animal on land or the contrail of an airplane in the sky.","A route for travel over land, especially a narrow, unpaved pathway for use by hikers, horseback riders, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/trail"}],"entries":[{"title":"Trail","blocks":[{"text":"A trail, also known as a path or track, is a dirt road or a small paved road, usually through an undeveloped natural area, that is designed primarily for use by pedestrians, equestrians and non-motorized vehicles, although occasionally some off-roading motor vehicles may also use it. In the United Kingdom and Ireland, footpath or hiking trail is the preferred term. However, it is sometimes applied to highways in North America. In the United States, the term was historically used to describe the westward expansion trails, which are land routes into or through wildness regions of the American West used by explorers and migrants."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Trail_between_two_fields_%28Slovenia%2C_Selo_pri_Mirni%29.jpg/330px-Trail_between_two_fields_%28Slovenia%2C_Selo_pri_Mirni%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trail","wikidata":"Q628179"},{"title":"Hiking","blocks":[{"text":"A hike is a long, vigorous walk, usually on trails or footpaths in the countryside. Walking for pleasure developed in Europe during the eighteenth century. Long hikes as part of a religious pilgrimage have existed for a much longer time. \"Hiking\" is the preferred term in Canada and the United States; the term \"walking\" is used in these countries for shorter, particularly urban walks. In the United Kingdom and Ireland, the word \"walking\" describes all forms of walking, whether it is a walk in a park or backpacking in the Alps. The word hiking is also often used in the UK, along with rambling, hillwalking, and fell walking. The term bushwalking is endemic to Australia. In New Zealand a long, vigorous walk or hike is called tramping. It is a common activity with numerous hiking organizations worldwide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Hiking_to_the_Ice_Lakes._San_Juan_National_Forest%2C_Colorado.jpg/330px-Hiking_to_the_Ice_Lakes._San_Juan_National_Forest%2C_Colorado.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hiking","wikidata":"Q12014035"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/MountainBikeTrail.jpg/500px-MountainBikeTrail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/MountainBikeTrail.jpg/1280px-MountainBikeTrail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/MountainBikeTrail.jpg/500px-MountainBikeTrail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mountain bike trail in the Forest of Dean, England","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMountainBikeTrail.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Kruununpuisto_Imatra.JPG/500px-Kruununpuisto_Imatra.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.